A wave of videos is spreading across social media under the hashtag #IAmJaneDoe to express solidarity with an anonymous Cornell University student who alleges she was sexually assaulted by several members of a fraternity in 2024.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f728814-c32c-11f1-9254-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/minibis.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9f728814-c32c-11f1-9254-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/minibis.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f2d3cd38-c32c-11f1-849b-bffe1abce33e</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 15:34:37 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Democratic party sues Trump administration over taxpayer-funded TV 'propaganda'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261007-dnc-sues-trump-administration-taxpayer-funded-tv-ads</link><description>
The Democratic National Committee sued US President Donald Trump’s administration on Wednesday over taxpayer-funded television ads promoting his political message, alleging illegal government propaganda. The lawsuit seeks to halt federal funding for the ads, which have cost more than $12 million to air ahead of November’s midterm elections, according to AdImpact.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fae7a376-c21b-11f1-8c0b-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/9939fec8f8dddd1013f1f9123540d7ad7a9e14b8.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fae7a376-c21b-11f1-8c0b-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/9939fec8f8dddd1013f1f9123540d7ad7a9e14b8.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1dfc4dc8-c29c-11f1-93c5-85fcfd3e8cdf</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 22:27:23 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/fae7a376-c21b-11f1-8c0b-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/9939fec8f8dddd1013f1f9123540d7ad7a9e14b8.jpg">© Kent Nishimura, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Thousands protest Brazilian presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro, fearing far-right return</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261007-thousands-protest-flavio-bolsonaro-far-right-brazil</link><description>
Thousands of people joined a student march in downtown Rio de Janeiro on Wednesday to protest against presidential candidate Flavio Bolsonaro ahead of Brazil's October 25 presidential runoff. Bolsonaro led Sunday's first-round vote, prompting demonstrators to rally behind incumbent President Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva and oppose the far right's return to power.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/16ee0312-c28f-11f1-a141-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/99c45d97550e9fe2e25c9fba173e4f6892b7e44f.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/16ee0312-c28f-11f1-a141-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/99c45d97550e9fe2e25c9fba173e4f6892b7e44f.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">44bb3390-c295-11f1-b69e-d51e1b7aff8b</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 22:01:02 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/16ee0312-c28f-11f1-a141-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/99c45d97550e9fe2e25c9fba173e4f6892b7e44f.jpg">© Mauro Pimentel, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Truth or Fake</category><title>White House promotes 'Trump TV' with AI-altered photo</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/white-house-promotes-trump-tv-with-an-ai-altered-photo</link><description>
The White House has promoted "Trump TV" using an AI-altered photo of passengers apparently watching the president's new streaming channel. But the image was originally posted online in February 2023, showing passengers watching the NFL Super Bowl, with one watching the film "Hitch". The original photo was modified using AI to make it appear as if the passengers were watching Trump TV. The post did not discalose that the image had been altered. Vedika Bahl explains in Truth or Fake.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3bd673f8-c285-11f1-9e85-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-18849501526ac69d4b32a593-88115309.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/3bd673f8-c285-11f1-9e85-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-18849501526ac69d4b32a593-88115309.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e97d4ef6-c284-11f1-a718-2380522e793b</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 19:45:24 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Vedika BAHL</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>From the Acropolis, Rubio calls on Europe to emerge from ‘slumber’</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261007-rubio-urges-europe-to-follow-trump-s-america-in-pursuit-of-national-power</link><description>
US Secretary of State Marco Rubio on Wednesday urged Western nations to return to what he called their civilisational roots and ensure the West "maintains its place at the head of the world" in a speech delivered against the backdrop of the Acropolis in Athens. The top US diplomat called on Europe to follow President Donald Trump's America in choosing the "unapologetic pursuit of national power" over the "global community".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/73023f52-c289-11f1-9563-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-07T155437Z-1531750308-RC2FYNASLNO8-RTRMADP-3-USA-RUBIO-EUROPE-GREECE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/73023f52-c289-11f1-9563-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-07T155437Z-1531750308-RC2FYNASLNO8-RTRMADP-3-USA-RUBIO-EUROPE-GREECE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">85e52cb2-c223-11f1-bb86-3bceda5c0f94</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 07:48:44 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/73023f52-c289-11f1-9563-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-07T155437Z-1531750308-RC2FYNASLNO8-RTRMADP-3-USA-RUBIO-EUROPE-GREECE.jpg">© Mark Schiefelbein, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Haiti postpones elections indefinitely amid gang violence</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261007-haiti-postpones-elections-indefinitely-as-gang-violence-hampers-preparations</link><description>
Haiti has indefinitely postponed elections scheduled for December and February, the electoral council said Tuesday, citing technical and operational requirements as gang violence continues to disrupt preparations. The delay leaves Haitians without a democratic process as authorities struggle to register voters and secure areas needed for the poll.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0cfb6390-c1e5-11f1-9b8e-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-29T174335Z-1383141441-RC25BNAS7IYK-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-UN.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0cfb6390-c1e5-11f1-9b8e-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-29T174335Z-1383141441-RC25BNAS7IYK-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-UN.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4a432782-c1eb-11f1-a39d-cf3290c41ac8</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 01:06:11 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/0cfb6390-c1e5-11f1-9b8e-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-29T174335Z-1383141441-RC25BNAS7IYK-RTRMADP-3-HAITI-VIOLENCE-UN.jpg">© Patrice Noel, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Americas</category><title>Christa Pike 'angry and confused' after surviving Tennessee execution attempt</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261006-christa-pike-regains-consciousness-after-botched-tennessee-execution-attempt</link><description>
Christa Pike is "angry and confused" after regaining consciousness almost six days after Tennessee's failed attempt to execute her by lethal injection, one of her attorneys said Wednesday. Pike, the first woman the state has attempted to put to death in 200 years, remains hospitalised with severe injuries to both arms.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82a77354-c1d1-11f1-8e5c-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26268720006166.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82a77354-c1d1-11f1-8e5c-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26268720006166.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e48db7fc-c1dd-11f1-96e3-137654958d82</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 23:30:18 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/82a77354-c1d1-11f1-8e5c-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26268720006166.jpg">© Via AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Colossal failure: Death penalty system in America is a broken process from start to finish'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/colossal-failure-death-penalty-system-in-america-is-a-broken-process-from-start-to-finish</link><description>
Jean-Emile Jammine is pleased to welcome Reed Brody, international human rights lawyer and former New York assistant attorney general. According to Brody, the botched execution of Christa Pike reflects a much larger problem: the relationship between capital punishment, institutional failure, due process, and human dignity. The technical failure of lethal injection exposes weaknesses throughout the legal architecture surrounding the death penalty, from the quality and adequacy of defense representation to the profound disparities between jurisdictions and the difficulties states face in administering executions. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d7328256-c1a9-11f1-bbbf-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261002-210915-211550-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d7328256-c1a9-11f1-bbbf-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261002-210915-211550-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">dc45820c-c1a9-11f1-b04e-2f0c78429203</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:50:00 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>A propos</category><title>Brazil 'has moved to the right progressively over the last two decades', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/a-propos/20261006-brazil-has-moved-to-the-right-progressively-over-the-last-two-decades-expert-says</link><description>
Senator Flavio Bolsonaro received on Monday endorsements ​from Ronaldo Caiado and Romeu Zema, two right-wing politicians who ran against him in Sunday's first-round vote, ​for ‌the runoff against President Luiz ⁠Inacio Lula da Silva later this month. Speaking with FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney, Anthony Pereira, Executive Director of the Stone Center for Latin American Studies at Tulane University, says that "Lula's path to victory lies in mobilising voters who didn't vote in the first round" while Flavio Bolsonaro "will probably benefit from the voters who voted for other right-wing candidates and who will simply migrate to him, so it looks a lot easier for Flavio to win the second round than it does for Lula".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d892e40-c1a6-11f1-b146-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-223111-224225-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d892e40-c1a6-11f1-b146-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-223111-224225-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">28cc76a0-c1a8-11f1-8dc1-178bc905adef</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:05:39 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Sharon GAFFNEY</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Brazil 'has moved to the right progressively over the last two decades', expert says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/brazil-has-moved-to-the-right-progressively-over-the-last-two-decades-expert-says</link><description>
Senator Flavio Bolsonaro received on Monday endorsements ​from Ronaldo Caiado and Romeu Zema, two right-wing politicians who ran against him in Sunday's first-round vote, ​for ‌the runoff against President Luiz ⁠Inacio Lula da Silva later this month. Speaking with FRANCE 24's Sharon Gaffney, Anthony Pereira, Executive Director of the Stone Center for Latin American Studies at Tulane University, says that "Lula's path to victory lies in mobilising voters who didn't vote in the first round" while Flavio Bolsonaro "will probably benefit from the voters who voted for other right-wing candidates and who will simply migrate to him, so it looks a lot easier for Flavio to win the second round than it does for Lula".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d892e40-c1a6-11f1-b146-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-223111-224225-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9d892e40-c1a6-11f1-b146-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261005-223111-224225-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a24ccc34-c1a6-11f1-a3b2-d7b89b0ca66e</guid><pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:03:02 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item></channel></rss>
